{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "COVID-19 is caused by Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2 (SARS-Cov-2) [1]. Since the observation of the first patients with COVID-19 in Wuhan in 2019, the disease quickly became a global pandemic [2]. As of September 6 th , 2021 more than 221 million individuals developed COVID-19 and 4.6 million died globally [2]. Currently, there are only a few interventions hat seem able to benefit the clinical course of COVID-19 [3]. Therefore, preventive measures are of vital importance to control COVID-19. The scientific, medical, and industrial communities have embarked on efforts to develop safe vaccines [4, 5]. At September 13 th , 2021 about 104 vaccines are being tested in humans, with 33 having reached phase III trials [6]. Of these, five vaccines were abandoned due to either lack of response or an undesirable adverse event (e.g., tested false positive for HIV), eight have been approved for full use, and eleven have been authorized for early or limited use [6]. With the rapid development, approval of vaccines for COVID-19, and the growing number of viral variants, there is a need for systematic reviews critically appraising the topic.",
      "reason": "Background and introduction describe the current global burden, the limited interventions, the number/status of vaccine candidates, and note uncertainties (rapid development, variants) establishing current knowledge and uncertainties."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "With the rapid development, approval of vaccines for COVID-19, and the growing number of viral variants, there is a need for systematic reviews critically appraising the topic. We recently published the first version of our review as a preprint, showing effects of mRNA and viral vector vaccines [7]. The aim of this second version of our living systematic review is to assess the effectiveness and safety of COVID-19 vaccines through analyses of all currently available randomized clinical trials.",
      "reason": "The text explicitly states the need for systematic reviews given rapid vaccine development and variants, and states the aim to assess effectiveness and safety, explaining the importance and need for the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "We recently published the first version of our review as a preprint, showing effects of mRNA and viral vector vaccines [7]. The aim of this second version of our living systematic review is to assess the effectiveness and safety of COVID-19 vaccines through analyses of all currently available randomized clinical trials.",
      "reason": "The authors acknowledge a prior version of their review (cited as a preprint) and state that this is the second version of their living systematic review, thereby explaining the need for this updated review in relation to previous work."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No text in the retrieved evidence describes the proposed mechanism of action of the vaccines; the introduction/background notes vaccine development but does not explain biological mechanisms."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review concerns standard vaccine interventions rather than complex multi-component delivery contexts; no evidence suggests the review addresses complex interventions or delivery contexts requiring a logic model."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
